{
  "schema_version": 1,
  "list_id": "claude-opus-5-5",
  "run": 1,
  "simulation_id": "sim-02",
  "member_id": "mehlig-bernhard",
  "model": "gpt-6-luna",
  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_luna_round2_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B023",
        "laureates": [
          "Antoine Georges",
          "Dieter Vollhardt",
          "Gabriel Kotliar"
        ],
        "citation": "For the development of dynamical mean-field theory of strongly correlated electrons."
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B012",
    "B002"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "I retain B023. Dynamical mean-field theory made a difficult class of interacting-electron problems tractable through a general, nonperturbative framework. Its decades of use and engagement with experiment support both its maturity and lasting scientific importance. Its contribution is methodological rather than a single material prediction, but that does not diminish the achievement: it changed how researchers can study correlated electrons. Georges, Vollhardt, and Kotliar remain my proposed laureates as central contributors to the framework's development and establishment.",
    "case_against": "The main difficulty remains exact credit. A framework develops through sustained work, and its applications and extensions involve a broad community. The historical record should clarify each proposed laureate's foundational role and distinguish the original development from later work. The absence of one uniquely decisive experimental result also makes the achievement's boundaries less immediate than for a specific prediction and realization.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "eriksson-olle",
        "point": "Eriksson recognizes DMFT's broad use and maturity but questions whether its individual credit can be assigned precisely and whether it has a uniquely decisive prediction.",
        "response": "I agree that attribution is the central question, and that the framework should not be presented as if it were one prediction. I still consider its general formulation a coherent achievement: its importance lies in making strongly correlated electron problems tractable across materials. I would retain the three names only if historical review substantiates their distinct foundational roles."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "johansson-goran",
        "point": "Johansson argues that circuit QED has an established scientific contribution independent of scalable quantum computing, while noting that its breadth and the three-person limit raise a serious question about Girvin's contribution.",
        "response": "The distinction between a mature scientific advance and its unfinished applications is useful for evaluating DMFT as well. Circuit QED has a compelling experimental platform case, but the question of fitting essential contributions within the laureate limit is substantial. DMFT's proposed set also requires careful historical scrutiny, yet the three names are linked to the framework itself, and I continue to find its well-established theoretical advance compelling."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "olsson-eva",
        "point": "Olsson favors optical lattice clocks for their replicated precision measurement and compact attribution, while stressing that credit should focus on foundational work rather than later performance gains.",
        "response": "I regard B012 as the strongest alternative. Its demonstrated precision, independent realization, and two-person attribution make its maturity and credit comparatively clear. I retain B023 because a general framework that reshapes the study of correlated materials is also a substantial physics achievement, even though its boundary and individual contributions need more historical examination."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "The historical division of foundational credit among Georges, Vollhardt, and Kotliar should be checked against the specific formulation and early establishment of DMFT.",
      "The scope should distinguish the framework's foundational development from its broader extensions and applications."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "My profile describes Johansson and Olsson as colleagues in the same Gothenburg physics environment and notes that the Gothenburg and Chalmers physics departments are closely linked. No specific collaboration is recorded. This institutional proximity may warrant review; no connection to the proposed laureates is apparent from the supplied profile."
  }
}
